---
source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Launch a test run

Example:
  snouty launch --launcher basic_test --config ./config \
    --test-name "my-test" \
    --description "nightly test run" \
    --duration 30 \
    --recipients "team@example.com"

The -c/--config flag points at a local directory containing docker-compose.yaml
(this is the config image source, unrelated to snouty's own settings file). By
default, every image that the run needs must be in the local image store, and
snouty pushes them automatically. Add --allow-remote-images to skip this check
for images that are not on this machine.

Alternatively, pass a pre-built config image directly:
  snouty launch --launcher basic_test \
    --config-image us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/proj/repo/config:latest \
    --duration 30

Extra parameters can be passed with --param:
  snouty launch --launcher basic_test --config ./config --duration 30 \
    --param antithesis.integrations.github.token=TOKEN \
    --param my.custom.property=value

User-defined attributes are params with an `attrs.` prefix. The server records
them on the run and `snouty runs show` lists them:
  snouty launch --launcher basic_test --config ./config --duration 30 \
    --param attrs.team=payments \
    --param attrs.branch=main \
    --param attrs.build=12345

When the config dir (or the current directory, without --config) is in a git
checkout, the launch records the checked-out commit and branch in run metadata.
For a github.com `origin` remote, it also records the repository and a link to
the commit. `snouty runs show` lists them under Version Control. A pre-built
config image (--config-image or --param antithesis.config_image) turns this off.

Additional container images that the config parser can't discover (e.g. an image
referenced only in a Kubernetes CRD field) can be registered with the
antithesis.images param, a semicolon-delimited [REGISTRY/]NAME(:TAG|@DIGEST)
list:
  snouty launch --launcher basic_k8s_test --config ./config --duration 30 \
    --param 'antithesis.images=app@sha256:...;db:latest'

Add --json for machine-readable output. The launch response prints as one JSON
object:
  snouty launch --json --launcher basic_test --config ./config --duration 30 |
    jq -r .runId

Next, wait for the run to finish with `snouty runs wait <run_id>`. The run ID is
the `run_id` value in the launch output, or `.runId` in the --json output. To
watch it live, use `snouty runs stream <run_id>`.

Credentials come from `snouty login` or from the environment variables below.
Tenant and repository may be set via the environment variables below, or in a
settings file (./.snouty.toml by default; see the global --settings/--profile
flags and the README). Environment variables take precedence.

Environment variables (override any settings file):
  ANTITHESIS_TENANT       Your Antithesis tenant name.
  ANTITHESIS_API_KEY      API key authentication.
  ANTITHESIS_USERNAME     Username (deprecated).
  ANTITHESIS_PASSWORD     Password (deprecated).
  ANTITHESIS_REPOSITORY   Container registry for pushing images.
  SNOUTY_CONTAINER_ENGINE Force "docker" or "podman" (auto-detected by default).

Usage: snouty launch [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -l, --launcher <LAUNCHER>
          Launcher name (e.g., basic_test, basic_k8s_test)
          
          [default: basic_test]

  -c, --config <CONFIG>
          Local config dir (docker-compose.yaml or a manifests/ subdir),
          auto-built and pushed as the config image

      --config-image <CONFIG_IMAGE>
          Pre-built config image reference (e.g.,
          us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/proj/repo/config:latest)

      --allow-remote-images
          Launch a compose config even when some of its images are not in the
          local image store. snouty will be unable to validate the image and pin
          it to an exact digest. Has no effect on a Kubernetes config

      --test-name <TEST_NAME>
          Test name

      --description <DESCRIPTION>
          Test description

      --duration <DURATION>
          Test duration in minutes, or h/m units (e.g. 90m, 2h, 1h30m); at least
          1 minute

      --ephemeral
          Mark the test run as ephemeral, so it does not appear in future
          reports as a historic result. A launch without --source is ephemeral
          already

      --source <SOURCE>
          Identifier that groups property history in reports — runs sharing a
          --source share history (e.g. per-branch)

      --recipients <RECIPIENTS>
          Report recipients (semicolon-delimited email addresses)

      --filter-logs-matching <FILTER_LOGS_MATCHING>
          Suppress log lines matching this RE2 pattern during fuzzing. Matching
          is unanchored and case-sensitive by default (use `(?i)` for
          case-insensitive). Suppressed lines stay available in multiverse
          debugging. RE2 syntax: no lookahead/lookbehind or backreferences. Max
          1023 bytes. Requires tenant release 59 or newer

      --filter-source-matching <FILTER_SOURCE_MATCHING>
          Suppress all output from log sources matching this RE2 pattern during
          fuzzing. A source is usually the name of the process that wrote the
          line, as shown in its `source` field. Matching is unanchored and
          case-sensitive (use `^name$` to match one source exactly). Suppressed
          output stays available in multiverse debugging. RE2 syntax: no
          lookahead/lookbehind or backreferences. Max 1023 bytes

      --performance-tier <PERFORMANCE_TIER>
          Performance tier for the run. Higher tiers explore system states
          faster through more parallelism. They also consume core hours faster.
          The server default is `standard`. To send a tier that is not listed,
          use `--param=antithesis.performance_tier=TIER`
          
          [possible values: standard, fast, turbo]

      --fault-tolerance <FAULT_TOLERANCE>
          Which faults to inject, by how much disruption the system must
          survive. `none`: no faults. `resilient`: degraded network links,
          thread pauses, container pauses of up to 10 seconds, CPU throttling
          and clock jitter. `high_availability`: everything in `resilient`, plus
          network partitions and killed or stopped containers or pods. `legacy`:
          all network faults and thread pausing, for a custom notebook only. To
          send a level that is not listed, use
          `--param=antithesis.fault_tolerance=LEVEL`
          
          [possible values: none, resilient, high_availability, legacy]

      --fault-exclusions <FAULT_EXCLUSIONS>
          Regular expression for the containers (or pods, on Kubernetes) that
          faults must not touch, matched against the container name. Thread
          pausing is machine-wide, so it still applies. Rust regex syntax: no
          lookaround or backreferences. Max 1024 characters

      --no-vcs
          Do not record the commit, branch, and repository of the local git
          checkout in run metadata. A pre-built config image (--config-image)
          also turns the detection off

      --param <PARAMS>
          Extra parameters as key=value pairs (repeatable)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Output JSON where supported (NDJSON for list/stream commands, pretty
          JSON otherwise)

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
